tion  panel b blinded assessment of outcome  and panel c  sample size calculation and this was  designed done across  different disease areas for in vivo  research and then the bottom right is a  list of the disease areas and on the  graphs they go on the x-axis from left  to right in that same order  and you can see that in panel a with  randomization although it did vary  across disease areas on average about a  quarter of the paper's  reported randomization and in panel b  although it did vary across disease  areas again  just under a third on average reported  blinding in the research publications  and you can see in panel c sample size  calculation that is quite consistent  across the board and less than one  percent of the papers studied  showed any justification for the numbers  of animal used  and you might think this depends on  where you publish and that papers and  high impact factor journals will publish  more rigorous science  but their study also looked at this and  they found that it doesn't make a  difference so if we look here panel a  again is looking at randomization panel  b  at blinded assessment of outcome and  panel c sample size calculation  and as before the percentage of papers  reporting these are on the y  axis but on the x axis here we have  deciles of journal impact factor so it's  increasing  from left to right and you can see that  there's no correlation between  the higher impact factor journals and  higher reporting of measures of rigor  so this really highlights the problem  with the reliability of animal research  and whether we can actually trust the  results that are being published in the  literature  and this is also an ethical problem  because if studies are not designed to  yield robust results  or if they're not reported properly then  it is a waste of animals  and it is an ethical issue but it can  also be an economic issue  so this paper was published a few years  ago and it looked at how much all of  this irreproducible research was costing  so the budget for pre-clinical research  is about 56 billion  a year in the us and they estimated in  this study that about  half of that was producing  irreproducible research  and then they looked at within the  irreproducible research  what were the categories of  irreproducibility and they found that  about half of that was down to the study  design  the analysis of the data or the  reporting and those are things that our  program of work at the nc3r specifically  focuses on  so the experimental design program at  the nc3rs aims to help researchers  improve the design of animal research  and its reporting in scientific  publications  so our perspective is that if studies  are not designed to yield reliable  results or if they're not reported in  enough detail  they can't add to the evidence base and  that means they're a waste of animals  so we've developed a number of resources  to help support scientists  in designing their experiments more  robustly and reporting them more  thoroughly  and today i'm going to talk to you about  the experimental design assistant  so the experimental design assistant or  eda is an online tool for researchers  to design and vivo experiments so it's  freely available  and it's available on eda.nc3rs.org.uk  and the eda is like having your own  personal statistical assistant  so it provides bespoke advice and  feedback on your experimental plans  it gives you recommendations on  statistical tests that are appropriate  for the design of your study  and it helps determine the most  appropriate sample size for your study  and provide dedicated support for  randomization and blinding  and you can produce a report to  summarize the key information in your  experimental design as a pdf  so in the eda each experimental plan is  represented as a diagram made up of  colored shapes called nodes  and each node represents a different  part of your experiment  so on the slide here we have a simple  two group comparison and i'm just going  to talk you through this diagram  so we start with a pool of animals and  the animals are allocated by complete  randomization into two groups  group one receives an injection of  vehicle and group two receives an  injection of a drug  and then we measure the plasma glucose  and all of the animals in our experiment  and the data collected so the plasma  glucose levels are used in the analysis  as well as the independent variable of  interest which in this case is whether  the animals received a vehicle or drug  so an eda diagram has three kind of main  types of information in it so in the  gray nodes in the top left here  we have high level information about the  experiment and the objectives and things  like that  in the blue and purple nodes here we  have the practical steps so these are  the things that you physically carry out  in the lab  and in the kind of green and maroon  color we have the analysis plan so  things are important when you're  analyzing your data  so the there is more information inside  each of these shapes  so for example in the group nodes you'll  have the number of animals per group  the number of experimental units per  group and justification for the sample  size  and then in the allocation node you'll  have more information about the  procedure for randomization so let's  have a look inside an allocation node  so we can see the randomization strategy  so you can select from your drop down  list in this case it's complete  randomization  but you can also name the randomization  procedure so that's how the random  sequence was generated so in this case a  spreadsheet generated by the eda  and you can see that there are other  fields inside the property of this note  so you can put more information inside  each of these shapes in the experiment  an eda diagram can be more explicit than  a narrative text description of an  experiment  because you've got more information  inside each of those components  and a good example of this is blinding  so often in a text description and  experiment you'll say the researchers  were blinded  but in an eda diagram you can say that  the researchers whether or not they were  blinded at the allocation stage  when you're taking the measurements and  during the analysis and not only whether  or not they were blinded but how that  blinding was carried out so for example  at the measurement stage  whether the animals coded or whether  somebody else took the measurements  who's not involved in the experiment so  you can be really explicit about what's  going on  and you can represent any experiment as  a diagram in the eda  and the diagrams are machine readable so  the system uses computer-based logical  reasoning to provide feedback  so some examples of the feedback include  this prompt which says  that the diagram does not include any  nuisance variables  so the prompt includes what a nuisance  variable is common types of nuisance  variables and animal experiments such as  baseline body weight or the day of the  experiment of this day-to-day  variability  and also how to use that variable in the  design and analysis of the experiment to  try and mitigate against the effects  another feedback prompt asks you to  clarify the blinding status when the  results are assessed  the prompt will explain why the blinding  is important different stages of the  experiment  during which you can be blinded and  different ways to blind different stages  of the experiment so you can decide not  only what is most appropriate for your  experiment  but what is actually feasible in the  facility or the laboratory that you're  working  so you can also use the eda to help  communicate your plans with others such  as an ethical review board or a funding  body  so the eda report was designed to help  researchers who are already entering  this information into the eda  to export key information at the click  of a button and use it to identify  this key information in the grant  application or ethical review process  so the report contains the objectives  and hypotheses of your experiment  the number and of animals you're using  and the justification for the sample  size  any steps you're taking to minimize bias  such as randomization and blinding  what your primary outcome is and  secondary outcomes if you're measuring  more than one outcome  and your plan statistical analysis  methods and then there's a summary of  the eda feedback that has not been  addressed  and an image of your eda diagram at the  end  so we've come up with this eda workflow  to help you get the most out of support  we provide in the eda  so what we suggest you do is you draw  your diagram and then you fill in the  information inside each  of the nodes so fill in the node  properties but if you're not sure what  to put in any of those fields then we  suggest that you leave it blank because  the eda can provide feedback to help you  decide what's most appropriate  so once you've had a first go at drawing  your diagram you can use the critique  function  and then it will give you some feedback  and you may go back and decide to redraw  parts of your diagram change it a bit  add more information in the node  properties now that you've read the  feedback  and then you can critique your diagram  again so you can repeat these first  three steps  as many times as you like until you're  happy with your experimental plan  then you can use the analysis suggestion  function and that will  recommend an analysis method that is  appropriate for the particular design  that you've put in the eda  and once you've done that you can use  the inbuilt power calculators to  determine the most appropriate sample  size  you can use the randomized allocation  report function to generate your  randomization sequence  and what this does is it generates a  randomization sequence taking into  account any blocking factors  and it generates that it's an excel  spreadsheet attached to an email  so the system will ask you to provide  the email address of the person who's  helping you with randomization and  blinding so the random allocation  sequence gets sent directly to them so  you can maintain  your blinding throughout the experiment  you can also share the experiment with  others so share your diagram with  colleagues and collaborators  and you can produce this um diagram  report the pdf we talked about in the  previous slide  and once you've carried out your  experiment you can go ahead and update  the diagram  so there are a couple of fields that are  specifically for once your drag  once your experiment has been finished  so for example in the group node there  is  a space to say the planned number of  experimental units  but also the actual number that you've  used so if you had some attrition during  the course of your experiment you could  record that  and that's really good because you can  hang on to that record of attrition  and then if you come to do a similar  study in the future you can look back  and you can go right okay i had 10  nutrition so you'll take that into  account in your next sample size  calculation  there's also more information on  experimental design on the eda website  so this covers concepts crucial to the  internal validity of your experiment  and how best to represent these in your  eda diagram  so an example of this is in our animal  characteristics page where we provide  guidance on how differences in animal  characteristics can influence the  experimental design used  so an example of this guidance is  regarding using both sexes and  experiments  so using both male and female animals in  an experiment enables the findings to be  generalized across the whole population  so you're not just restricted to one sex  but one of the reasons researchers don't  always do this is that they believe that  the variation in female  animals especially across the eastern  cycle can introduce extra variability  so this graph on this slide is plotting  the coefficient of variation for  different experiments from a  meta-analysis study  so this study looked at 293 articles  with behavioral morphological  physiological and molecular traits that  were monitored in male and female mice  and these female mice were tested  without regards to the estrous cycle so  they didn't control for that  and you can see that by plotting the  variability so towards the right hand  side of the x-axis means more  variability  and the data from the females is plotted  in red and the data from the males is  plotted in blue  and where they overlap it is purple and  you can see that the variability was not  greater than females than a male so  that's not a good reason  to not include females in your study so  this graph is from a different study  looking at sex bias and this time they  were looking at sex bias in pain  research  so the graph shows analysis of  preclinical studies published in the  journal pane  so the bar on the left covers research  published between 1996 and 2005.  and it shows that about 80 of the  studies in this period used  only males and then the other bars are  2015  16 17 18 and 19 across from left to  right  and it shows that there is over time the  male only studies have decreased as more  studies are conducted in either females  or  in both sexes and the studies including  both sexes are shown in this graph and  the three shades of purple  so these different shades represent how  the sex data were analyzed  so the darker purple where both sexes  were included but the sex differences  were not analyzed  and then the other two lighter shades of  purple indicate where sex differences  were looked for but either not  identified in the mid shade of purple  or a difference was reported in the  lightest shade of purple  so you can see that we are making  progress towards using both sexes  in our studies but there is still some  more work to be done  so it does make sense to include both  sexes in your study unless you are  studying a process like oogenesis that  does occur only in one sex  so using both sexes doesn't necessarily  mean you need to increase the total  number of animals in your experiment but  to ensure  that you have the same proportion of  males to females in each of your  experimental groups you should randomize  males and females separately to your  different intervention groups  and this prevents sex bias in those  groups  if you are using both sexes to make your  results more generalizable so you want  them to be generalizable across the  whole population  but you have good reason to believe that  you're not expecting a big difference  from males and females  then you don't need to increase the  total number of animals you're using  and you can determine the sample size by  powering for the biologically relevant  effect size of your main intervention  say your drug treatment  and use both sexes with preferably half  and half male and female in each group  and if you include sex and the analysis  is blocking factor  then this takes into account the  variability induced by sex  and increases your ability to detect a  real effect of that intervention  if you do want to compare male and  female animals to see if the  intervention affects them differently  you would design your study with two  factors of interest so the intervention  and sex and the number of animals you  need in your study depends on the  purpose and what comparisons  and which groups you're going to be  comparing afterwards  and so we do have some guidance on this  on our eda website  in the experimental design section and  the animal characteristics page  so why should you use the eda we believe  that using the eda  improves the reliability of your results  so it helps you address obvious sources  of bias  and this helps you address them while  you're planning your study so that means  that if you do  find a potential source of bias you can  do something about it and mitigate  against it so that you can fix it  before you've started your study so it's  not too late it can also help promote  better understanding of experimental  design  so raise awareness of why these things  are important so every time a user gets  feedback from the eda you learn  something new  and the system is not a black box  telling you exactly what to do but it  does provide information about the  implications of doing things a certain  way  so you can understand what you're doing  and make informed choices  the eda can also facilitate peer review  or assessment of your experimental plans  because it's such an explicit  description  then it can be really transparent so  editors peer reviewers and readers  having access to your design and all the  information  have a really good idea of knowing  exactly what you're doing  and also it can form a sort of soft  pre-registration so it can provide  evidence that your study was planned  as you're reporting it and you haven't  for example change your primary outcome  measure after looking at the data and  this is because the eda  report the pdf report i talked about  earlier when you create that it has a  time stamp on it  and if you download that report and then  maybe put it on something like open  science framework or another data  repository  you'd also have an upload timestamp for  that as well so you could have two  kind of ways to prove that you have  planned your study as you are reporting  it  the eda can also promote more careful  consideration of your experimental plans  so we'd really want scientists to spend  more time planning their experiments  identifying their variables considering  the data you're going to be collecting  so we want scientists to spend more time  planning and thinking about things  before they've started the study so if  you discover potential problems you can  mitigate against them or you can fix  them  rather than completing the study  realizing afterwards that you've  introduced biases but there's nothing  you can do about it  and we also think the diagrams are  really useful to help discuss  your experimental plans and lab meetings  or workshops because having a visual  overview of your experiment  makes it easier to discuss the specific  thing you want help with  like for example figuring out how people  are going to help you with the blinding  or discussing which is the best  primary outcome measure for your  specific scientific question  so the eda helps you design your  experiments but if you want guidance  when planning how to breed your  experimental animals then the nc3 ounce  has breeding and colony management  guidance online as well  so the guidance has been produced by an  international working group of experts  in colony management and experimental  design  including experts from the jackson lab  in the us and the mary lyon  in the uk and the center for  phenogenomics  in canada so the information is  available as a web resource  that includes best practice for managing  your colony considerations when  archiving your lines  and tips for selecting a breeding  strategy for example the things  you need to watch out for that may be  affected by the genotype or the  time frame when your animals are needed  do you need a certain number of animals  um every six months for example  or do you need a whole lot of animals  all in one go  it also includes a frequently asked  questions page which includes a worked  example to calculate  reading numbers we also have an email  advice service for  very specific questions for example if  you're having trouble breeding a  difficult line then you can email us  and we can forward that on to the expert  working group to get some help  we are having a webinar on efficient  reading on the 1st of march 2021 and if  you are interested in joining us  you can go to nc3rs.org dot uk webinars  and you'll find a way to register there  so the eda helps you plan experiments  and the breeding and colony management  guidance helps you  plan the breeding of your experimental  cohorts but we developed the  arrive guidelines to improve the  reporting of information and animal  research publications  so the arrived guidelines were  originally published in 2010 and i've  been revised over the last few years and  the updated guidelines arrived 2.0 were  released in july last year  so the guidelines consist of a checklist  of items that have to be included in  their manuscript to ensure comprehensive  and transparent reporting  so the items in the arrive guidelines  are important for different reasons  so for internal validity for example  description of study design how the  animals were allocated to groups how the  sample size was chosen  so this is really crucial information to  understand how reliable and robust  those results are some of the items are  important to determine that  to ensure the paper has enough  information to repeat the studies that  are described  so comprehensive description of  experimental procedures  and description of the characteristics  of the animals in this study  and then some items provide information  about the context and scientific  relevance of the study  so for example the scientific background  any relevance to other species  and information about their ethical  approvals so the arrive guidelines apply  to all in vivo research so it doesn't  matter if the species used does not  require ethical approval the guidelines  are about rigorous science so that  applies to all  and vivo experiments in arrive 2.0 the  items are organized into two sets  so the arrive essential 10 is the  minimum requirement so this means the  basic minimum to include in a manuscript  because without this information  reviewers and readers can't confidently  assess the reliability of the findings  presented  and then there's a recommended set so  these items complement the essential 10  and add really important context to the  study and reporting all items in both  lists  represents best practice and we have  introduced a couple of new items so for  example protocol registration  so pre-registration is now standard  practice for clinical trials but it's  also emerging as a mechanism that's  likely to improve the transparency of  animal research  and for that reason protocol  registration is one of the new items  that we added to the newly revised  arrive guidelines  so protocols can be registered in a  registry but another option is via  registered reports  so registered reports is a publishing  format where you have peer review of the  study design including your analysis  plan  before you start your study so this  means that reviewers comments and  suggestions can be taken into account  before you begin the practical  experiments  and this can really help you make your  research stronger so after this initial  review called stage one review you get  in principle acceptance of your study  so this means you can publish your study  regardless of whether your results are  statistically significant or not so this  really encourages more rigorous study  design  and helps reduce publication bias and  the registered reports format is used by  over 250 journals including bmc biology  royal society open science and plus  biology and i've put a link  to the registered report website on this  page so if you'd like to learn more  about them you can  check them out there another important  initiative i'd like to highlight is  reproducibility  so reproducibility is a grassroots  initiative helping researchers discuss  issues papers and ideas about improving  science reproducibility and the open  science movement  so it's open to researchers in any field  and any career stage so anyone can get  involved and anyone is welcome to start  their own reproducibility club  so if you're interested in finding out  more their website is  reproducibility.org  and they have starter packs on the open  science framework and i've put a link on  the slide  to their open science framework  repository so the resources on osf  include reading lists to help you get  started and there is one tailored  specifically towards in vivo studies  so i'd just like to conclude by  highlighting the reach of arrive and eda  and so they are endorsed by many of the  major stakeholders in scientific  research  so for example the arrive guidelines are  endorsed by over 1 000 journals  so they're recommended in their  instructions to authors and many major  funding organizations recommend that  researchers use the eda  when preparing a grant application that  contains animal research  so that includes the mrc and the welcome  trust in the uk  but also funders in the in europe or the  us like the nih  there's also grassroots support from the  researchers themselves so the eda has  over  10 000 registered users and over the  past few years we have had over 40 000  requests for printed copies of the  arrive guidelines from 50 different  countries  so thank you very much for your  attention if you'd like more information  about the eda you can go to  eda.nc3rs.org  and if you'd like more information about  the arrive guidelines the website is  arriveguidelines.org  thank you
 

